Description: Floating windows: snap to edges, corners and thirds, easier resize grab
 Windows-style window management for sway's floating windows, built into
 the compositor because sway's IPC reports no pointer position and no drag
 start or end, so no outside program can do it properly.
 .
 Snapping: drag a floating window (by its title bar, or with the floating
 modifier) to a screen edge or corner. A translucent preview in the theme's
 indicator colour shows where it will land; release to snap it to that
 half, quarter, or (top edge) the whole workspace. Bars and gaps are left
 clear. Dragging a snapped window away gives it back its old size.
 .
 Keyboard: `snap left|right|up|down` step like Windows' Win+Arrow. Left
 and right move a window one step toward that side: to the half, to the
 quarter in the same row, or from the other side back to the middle
 (restored). Pressed again on its own side, a window cycles half -> one
 third -> two thirds -> half; thirds step a column at a time. Up and down
 move within a side, half <-> top or bottom quarter; up from anywhere else
 maximizes, down from maximized restores. Sway has no minimize, so down on
 a bottom quarter or an unsnapped window does nothing. In full:
 .
   from           left             right             up         down
   (unsnapped)    left-half        right-half        max        -
   max            left-half        right-half        -          restore
   left-half      left-third       restore           top-left   bottom-left
   left-third     left-two-thirds  center-third      top-left   bottom-left
   left-2/3       left-half        restore           top-left   bottom-left
   center-third   left-third       right-third       max        restore
   top-left       left-half        top-right         max        left-half
   bottom-left    left-half        bottom-right      left-half  -
   (right-hand zones mirror the left-hand ones)
 .
 A third and the two thirds beside it fill the workspace exactly, with the
 inner gap between them, as two halves do. `snap next-output` moves the
 window to the next screen (sway's output order, wrapping) in the same
 zone, or at the same relative spot if it isn't snapped, and keeps its
 restore box with it; with one screen it does nothing.
 .
 Snap assist: floating_snap_assist names a program sway runs after every
 snap, key or drag, and after a restore, as `<program> <con_id> <zone>
 [<free zone>]` -- but only when the window has another floating window
 on its workspace. The free zone (left-half or right-half) is given when
 the window went to a half and no other snapped window is in the other
 half: the moment to offer the other windows for it. Without it, a picker
 the program left open is out of date and should close. The program snaps
 its pick with an absolute zone, which takes the free half, so the run
 that follows doesn't offer again. Off (none) by default.
 .
 Resize grab: floating windows get an invisible band around them that
 starts a resize, and the stretch of border near a corner counts as the
 corner. Without it the only grab area is the border itself.
 .
 New commands:
   floating_snap enable|disable     drag-to-edge snapping (default: enable)
   floating_grab_margin <px>        the resize band, 0-64 (default: 8)
   floating_snap_assist <program>|none
                                    run after a snap (default: none)
   snap left|right|up|down          step, as above
   snap next-output|prev-output     the same zone on another screen
   snap restore                     back to the box from before
   snap max|left-half|right-half|top-left|top-right|bottom-left|
        bottom-right|left-third|center-third|right-third|
        left-two-thirds|right-two-thirds
                                    straight to that zone
 The settings' defaults need no config lines, so Tebian's config has none
 that an unpatched sway would reject, except floating_snap_assist, which
 Tebian writes only while the installed sway accepts it. `snap` in a key
 binding is safe anywhere: sway checks a binding's command only when the
 key is pressed. Revision 1 of this patch had no up, down, thirds,
 outputs or assist, and its left and right always went to the half.
 Most code is in new files (tree/snap.c, commands/snap.c); the hooks in
 existing files are small, to keep the patch easy to carry to new
 releases.
 .
 Applies to SwayFX 0.6.
